COP4634 Project 2 - Multi-Threaded Collatz Stopping Time Generator
Lance Penaflor

Build and run
-------------
make
./mt-collatz N T [-nolock]

N is the inclusive upper bound, starting at 1. T is the number of worker
threads. Both must be positive decimal integers no larger than INT_MAX.
The tested experiment range is N=3000000. Sequence arithmetic uses unsigned
64-bit values and the histogram contains buckets 0..1000. Arbitrary inputs
whose sequences overflow 64 bits or exceed 1000 steps are not supported.
Thread counts must fit available system resources.

Example with separate output streams:
./mt-collatz 100000 4 > histogram.csv 2> timing.csv
Use >> and 2>> to append. Stdout contains 1001 stopping_time,frequency rows;
stderr contains N,T,seconds.nanoseconds with nine fractional digits. Timing
includes thread creation, computation, and joining, and excludes printing.

Default mode protects both the global work counter and histogram with a
mutex. -nolock intentionally removes synchronization to demonstrate races;
multithreaded output in that mode is not reliable.

Significant problems encountered
--------------------------------
Two shared resources needed protection: claiming the next starting number
and incrementing a histogram bucket. Both operations use the same mutex;
sequence computation occurs outside the critical sections. Without locks,
all 70 archived multithreaded experiment runs produced incorrect histograms.

The locked four-thread experiment included a 14.682816-second observation.
Its cause was not measured. It is retained in the ten-run mean rather than
silently removed; the report discusses the resulting variability.

Final review found that numeric conversion accepted suffixes and could throw
uncaught errors on malformed input. Complete decimal arguments are now
validated and conversion errors produce a diagnostic and nonzero exit.
The global histogram and worker comments now follow the assignment's
structure, and the Makefile explicitly links pthreads for Linux builds.
The supplied workbook had data but no embedded charts; the required native
Excel runtime chart and histogram have now been added.

GDB and Valgrind are not installed on this macOS host. LLDB successfully
stopped in worker() and showed four worker threads plus the main thread.
AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer checks passed; these checks
do not establish a clean Valgrind leak report or absence of data races.
Linux compilation, GDB, and Valgrind checks remain for the department server.

Deliverables and status
-----------------------
mt-collatz.cpp, Makefile, README, report.pdf, and cop4634 proj2.xlsx are
staged together in deliverables/. Upload individual files as required by
the assignment. The report draft follows Project 2 Midpoint Report Template.docx. Canvas submission has not been performed.

The workbook contains all histogram buckets, raw measurements, machine
specifications, averaged runtimes, and two embedded charts. Timing data is
from October 12, 2026; final checks are from October 16, 2026. The worker
algorithm is unchanged from the timing experiment.

Developer notes
---------------
python3 tools/verify.py
runs independent-reference histogram comparisons and input/output checks.
See VALIDATION.md for results and outstanding server checks.

report.md is the editable report source. To regenerate the PDF, install
reportlab in a Python environment, then run python tools/build_report.py.
To regenerate workbook charts, install openpyxl and run
python tools/add_workbook_charts.py. These libraries are not needed to
compile or run the C++ submission.
